    {
      "q": "How do I teach critical thinking to a 4-year-old without a screen?",
      "a": "Start with simple sorting games (like separating toys by color or size), matching activities, and open-ended building blocks. Ask 'why' questions during play, like 'Why do you think this block fits here?' to encourage reasoning."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is screen-free play important for developing reasoning skills?",
      "a": "Screen-free play requires active engagement with physical objects and social interaction. It forces kids to use hands-on trial and error, develop patience, and practice verbal reasoning — all of which build deeper neural connections than passive screen time."
    },
    {
      "q": "Can playing board games really improve my child's problem-solving abilities?",
      "a": "Yes. Board games like checkers, Clue, and cooperative games require planning, predicting outcomes, and adapting strategies — key problem-solving skills. Research shows that regular board game play enhances executive function and cognitive flexibility in children aged 4-12."
